C = [
("h1", "4. The Messenger Before the Lord: Malachi 3:1"),
("p", "Claim: Malachi speaks of a forerunner twice, and the two sayings are not the same saying. In 3:1 the LORD says, “I will send my messenger,” and the one who follows is the LORD himself, coming suddenly to his temple. In 4:5 the LORD says, “I will send you Elijah the prophet,” and what follows is the great and dreadful day. The Gospels quote the first and leave the second where Malachi put it. A reader who keeps the two apart can see why the Gospels can say that John fulfils the one and still leave the other open."),
("sub", "4.1 The first saying"),
("p", "“Behold, I will send my messenger, and he shall prepare the way before me: and the LORD, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple, even the messenger of the covenant, whom ye delight in: behold, he shall come, saith the LORD of hosts” (Malachi 3:1). The sentence has two persons in it. The first is “my messenger,” who prepares the way “before me,” and the speaker is the LORD of hosts. The second is “the LORD, whom ye seek,” who comes “to his temple,” and who is called “the messenger of the covenant.” The sentence promises a forerunner and the coming of the one he goes before, in one breath, and it names the place of the coming as the temple."),
("p", "The Hebrew behind “my messenger” is the same word as the name of the book. Malachi 1:1 reads, “The burden of the word of the LORD to Israel by Malachi,” and the name is the Hebrew form of “my messenger.” The volume notes the fact and does not build on it. The book does not say that Malachi is the messenger of 3:1, and the Gospels do not say so either; they apply the sentence to John."),
("sub", "4.2 What follows the coming"),
("p", "The verses after 3:1 describe what the LORD’s coming does. “But who may abide the day of his coming? and who shall stand when he appeareth? for he is like a refiner’s fire, and like fullers’ soap: and he shall sit as a refiner and purifier of silver: and he shall purify the sons of Levi, and purge them as gold and silver, that they may offer unto the LORD an offering in righteousness” (Malachi 3:2, 3). Then, “Then shall the offering of Judah and Jerusalem be pleasant unto the LORD, as in the days of old, and as in former years” (Malachi 3:4). And judgment: “And I will come near to you to judgment; and I will be a swift witness against the sorcerers, and against the adulterers, and against false swearers, and against those that oppress the hireling in his wages, the widow, and the fatherless” (Malachi 3:5)."),
("p", "The whole paragraph is addressed to the house of Levi and to Judah and Jerusalem, and it is about an offering made in righteousness at the temple. It is Israel’s paragraph. The second volume of this series reads the charges against the priests; this chapter needs only the point that the coming of 3:1 is described in terms of a refining, a purifying of the sons of Levi, and a judgment, and that the Gospels do not report these things done when John came."),
("sub", "4.3 The Gospels quote the first half"),
("p", "Three Gospels quote Malachi 3:1, and each stops in the same place. Mark opens: “As it is written in the prophets, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee” (Mark 1:2). The Lord says of John, “For this is he, of whom it is written, Behold, I send my messenger before thy face, which shall prepare thy way before thee” (Matthew 11:10; Luke 7:27). In all three the quotation ends at “before thee.” None of them carries on to “and the LORD, whom ye seek, shall suddenly come to his temple.”"),
("p", "Two differences from the Hebrew are visible in the Gospels’ wording. The Gospels have “before thy face” and “thy way before thee,” where Malachi has “before me.” The volume reports the difference and does not explain it. What the volume takes from it is only that the speaker in the Gospels is addressing the Lord Jesus, and the sentence the Gospels quote is the half of Malachi 3:1 that is about the forerunner. The half that is about the coming to the temple is not quoted in any of the three places, and the volume does not add it for them."),
("sub", "4.4 The two sayings side by side"),
("p", "Malachi 3:1 and 4:5 can be read as one saying about one person, or as two sayings about two. The text does not decide it on its face, and both readings have been held. What the text shows is a difference of wording and of what follows. In 3:1 the sender calls the figure “my messenger” and the figure prepares “the way before me.” In 4:5 he calls him “Elijah the prophet,” and he comes “before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.” In 3:1 what follows is the coming of the LORD to his temple. In 4:5 what follows is the day."),
("p", "Luke’s account of the angel’s word to Zacharias takes up both. Gabriel says of John, “he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elias, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for the Lord” (Luke 1:17). The sentence has the “go before” of 3:1, the “Elias” of 4:5, and the turning of the hearts of 4:6, and it does not say that John is Elias. It says that he goes “in the spirit and power of Elias.” The volume reads the sentence in chapter 6."),
("ctbl", "Malachi’s two sayings of a forerunner.", [
    ("", "Malachi 3:1", "Malachi 4:5, 6"),
    ("Who sends", "The LORD of hosts", "The LORD"),
    ("Who is sent", "“My messenger”", "“Elijah the prophet”"),
    ("What he does", "Prepares the way before me", "Turns the heart of the fathers to the children, and the children to the fathers"),
    ("What follows", "The LORD comes suddenly to his temple", "The great and dreadful day of the LORD"),
    ("What the Gospels quote", "Mark 1:2; Matthew 11:10; Luke 7:27 (to “before thee”)", "Matthew 11:14 and 17:11, 12 name Elias; Luke 1:17 echoes 4:6"),
  ], [0.2, 0.4, 0.4]),
("sub", "4.5 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Malachi 3:1 speaks of a messenger and of the LORD who comes suddenly to his temple after him; Malachi 4:5 speaks of Elijah the prophet and of the day.",
    "The Gospels quote Malachi 3:1 only as far as “before thee,” and apply it to John; they do not quote the half about the coming to the temple.",
    "The paragraph that follows Malachi 3:1 describes a refining of the sons of Levi and a judgment, and the Gospels do not report these done at John’s coming.",
    "Luke 1:17 joins the “go before” of 3:1, the name of 4:5 and the turning of 4:6, and says “in the spirit and power of Elias,” not “Elias.”",
    "Whether 3:1 and 4:5 speak of one figure or two is not settled by the wording alone; the volume goes on to read what each Gospel does with the names.",
  ]),
]
